{
  "analysis": "The review describes paid Poké Balls being consumed during capture attempts, with Pokémon repeatedly breaking out after the reviewer began purchasing them. The reviewer presents this as a hidden change or manipulation of capture outcomes designed to deplete the balls and cause further purchases, which is an allegation that the game determines outcomes unfairly rather than ordinary bad luck. The later edit says the reviewer did not write the review, but it does not describe any in-game messaging, posting, or account action by Pokémon GO, so it does not support Friend Spam / Impersonation.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Alleged rigging: assign where the span asserts that the game manipulates the determination of outcomes or tunes them to a hidden schedule.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer links purchasing Poké Balls to repeated failed capture outcomes and depletion, implying that the game is manipulating capture odds to encourage more play or purchases."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes slow progress through hard levels, saying it can take a week to pass them, but does not identify a repetitive activity or a game-imposed timer, so this does not support Grinding or Playing by Appointment. The reviewer also states that some tasks are impossible unless boosters are bought, which describes payment as the route to overcoming a progress obstacle.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Free alternative boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The span makes buying boosters the necessary alternative for completing otherwise impossible tasks and progressing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a time-limited event with a game-defined schedule. The player was three steps away from a 525-dice reward, had no dice left to continue, and saw that the event displayed two days remaining. After returning from sleep, the event had changed, so the player lost access to the nearly completed reward before the displayed window ended. This is a scheduled event and reward opportunity controlled by the game clock, not a daily login reward, grinding mechanic, or explicit social pressure. The review does not clearly describe a bug or technical fault; it presents the behavior as part of the game design.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Fear of Missing Out: where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment.",
      "rationale": "The event's game-controlled schedule changed the available reward opportunity despite the displayed two-day window, requiring play according to the game's timing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes three game mechanics. First, the game links rewards to inviting friends and getting them to join through Facebook, which uses social recruitment as an incentive. Second, the player spends a stock of in-game dice by playing at the maximum bet and relies on luck for outcomes such as shutdowns and bank heists; this is a chance-based wager using earned in-game currency, so Gambling applies without Intermediate Currency because no real-money conversion is described. Third, the sticker system repeatedly gives duplicate stickers, describing unwanted repeated outcomes from a reward pool and supporting Reward Mania. The internet connection error is explicitly a technical fault, so it does not itself receive a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator: players are incentivized to invite friends to join the game for in-game rewards.",
      "rationale": "The game ties rewards to recruiting friends and having them join through Facebook."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK! No Shut downs and no bank heists.",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling boundary rule: assign Gambling when a chance mechanic uses a stake of earned in-game currency even without a real-money purchase being described.",
      "rationale": "The player wagers dice at maximum bet on luck-based outcomes that may produce shutdowns or bank heists."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way...",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards indicator and boundary rule: repeated duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomized reward pool support Reward Mania.",
      "rationale": "The sticker reward system repeatedly delivers unwanted duplicate items, making the delivery mechanism rather than the reward value the source of frustration."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a deliberate interface default: opening the game or the 1v1 area automatically moves to and selects the highest-bet table available for the player's coins. This directs the player toward the most costly table and is said to cause accidental mis-clicks that spend their accumulated coins. After those coins are lost, the reviewer says players are forced to buy more coins with real money, making payment a route to continued play. The 1v1 reference also describes player-versus-player competition. The review does not establish chance-based gambling, leftover currency waste, or an accidental real-money purchase itself; the accidental loss is of in-game coins.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "Exciting UI Effect / boundary rule for attention-directing interface design",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically directs the player toward the highest-cost betting option rather than a neutral or low-cost default."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "definition; payment is described as required to continue after an in-game currency barrier",
      "rationale": "The review states that losing the game's coins forces players to purchase more with real money in order to keep playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "currency-as-object boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The pronoun 'ones' refers to the game's coins, so the span describes acquiring in-game currency through a real-money purchase."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the 1v1 section",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "A 1v1 mode explicitly pits players against one another in competition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a perceived manipulation of random outcomes: the reviewer says the RNG is clearly working against the player, which is an allegation that the game determines outcomes unfairly rather than leaving them genuinely random. The review also describes a poor in-game economy: a level costs 2200 and a win barely returns that amount, while spending actual money is presented as the alternative. However, it does not explicitly state that payment is required to continue or progress, nor does it describe a currency conversion, a specific stranded shortfall, or a reward-delivery system such as loot boxes. No web search is needed because the mechanics are understandable from the review itself.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you it's crazy",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs alleged rigging: assign when the review asserts that the game manipulates or determines outcomes rather than leaving them genuinely random.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that the game's random outcome system is biased against the player, describing manipulated outcome determination rather than merely reporting an unlucky streak."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a base-building game as repetitive, but “Same old same old grinder” is only a general quality judgment with no named repeated task tied to progress, so it does not establish Grinding. The clear mechanic is monetized competitive advantage: the reviewer says winning the event requires spending money, explicitly characterizing the game as pay to win and reporting a very large expenditure to win. The event is not explicitly described as competition against other players, so Competition is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Calculated total spent on last event was 30k to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The span states that a large monetary expenditure was used to win an in-game event, describing payment as the route to a gameplay advantage or outcome."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a monetized advantage for paying players, which maps to Pay to Progress through the codebook's Pay to Win indicator. It also describes increasingly long base-development timers; the permitted search confirmed that Last War uses construction and building-upgrade timers, so this is a wait-to-play mechanic. The statement that everything scales endlessly and offers no meaningful endpoint describes an unattainable, continually receding progression path, supporting Infinite Treadmill, but the phrase \"just more grind\" does not name a specific repeated activity and is not enough for Grinding. The UI is explicitly said to be confusing and overwhelming for the purpose of making players buy things, which is UI Misdirection. Finally, the alliance passage describes players being set against one another through attacks, supporting Competition; it does not establish Forced Fellowship because the codebook excludes coerced grouping for protection when no obligation to group members is described. The search established that base buildings and upgrades in Last War use construction or upgrade timers. ([lastwar.pro](https://www.lastwar.pro/?utm_source=openai))",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes the game as giving paying players an advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game build or upgrade timers",
      "rationale": "Base development is governed by increasingly long timers that make the player wait before advancement."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to",
      "rule_applied": "Definition and boundary rule: progression continually expands with no attainable end state",
      "rationale": "Endless scaling and the absence of a meaningful endpoint describe a progression goal that continually recedes."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary rule: the interface is described as confusing for a purchase-directed purpose",
      "rationale": "The review attributes the UI's confusing presentation to directing players toward buying."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "Competition definition: the game makes players act against one another",
      "rationale": "Players are exposed to hostile attacks from other players or alliances, establishing adversarial competition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Last War: Survival Game base upgrade times construction timers",
  "search_result": "The search established that Last War buildings and base structures use construction and upgrade timers, confirming that the review's base update-time complaint refers to timed development waits. ([lastwar.pro](https://www.lastwar.pro/?utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two mechanics. First, the game has a daily-reward system that the player cannot complete or collect unless a daily wagering requirement is met, so the daily reward opportunity is conditional rather than freely available. Second, in the casino-slots context, betting 50,000 describes wagering in a chance-based game using an in-game balance; the review does not describe real money being directly used to place the wager, so Intermediate Currency is not supported. The statement about paying appears to express refusal to obtain more currency, but it does not clearly say that payment is required for overall game progression, so Pay to Progress is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "T_DailyRewards definition and indicator for daily task or reward systems with an active condition or consequence for obtaining the day's reward.",
      "rationale": "The player describes a daily reward system that cannot be completed or collected unless the required daily activity is performed."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "bet 50,000",
      "rule_applied": "M_Gambling boundary vs Reward Mania: a wager using paid or earned intermediate currency qualifies even when no real-money purchase is described.",
      "rationale": "In this casino-slots game, betting 50,000 describes staking in-game currency in a chance-based wager."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
