{
  "analysis": "The review describes difficult late-game progression in Gardenscapes. The player repeatedly runs out of lives while trying to clear levels, taking several days per level; this implies repeated attempts and tedious repetition to advance, supporting Grinding. The review also explicitly states that buying add-ons or paying is necessary to get through the levels, supporting Pay to Progress. The daily frequency is a report of how often lives are exhausted, not a daily reward or required appointment, and the money and time are described as wasted while uninstalling, so Easy to Get, Hard to Lose is not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "I run out of lives 3-4 times daily to beat every alternate level, avg 5-7 days per level.",
      "rule_applied": "Grinding boundary rule versus Slow Progress: the span describes repeated attempts and resource depletion required to beat successive levels, not merely that progress is slow.",
      "rationale": "The player repeatedly exhausts lives while attempting levels and must spend multiple days per level, indicating tedious repeated effort for progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "You must buy add ons else you can't make it through a sane mind.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Progress definition and Free alternative boundary: payment is described as necessary to overcome the progression obstacle.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly says that advancing through the game requires buying add-ons."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review contrasts misleading third-party advertising with the actual in-game structure. The ads are outside the installed game's design and therefore do not trigger T_Advertisement. In the game itself, the player must perform the strategy loop of building an army, training, and gathering resources before reaching the occasional puzzle content. The reviewer explicitly characterizes this required progress work as chores, describing tedious prerequisite activity rather than direct access to the advertised puzzles. There is no evidence of a daily schedule, an impossible end state, monetization, or social pressure.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "you'll be doing chores in order to get to them",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Boundary rule vs Gameplay described as repetitive",
      "rationale": "The span describes tedious prerequisite tasks that must be performed to make progress toward the puzzles, with the preceding context identifying army-building, training, and resource gathering as those tasks."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes misleading advertising for pin-pull levels and mini-games, but those advertisements are external marketing and are out of scope under R13. The reviewer also describes paying for extra in-game coins, which establishes real money being exchanged for virtual currency and supports Intermediate Currency. The high level number and long time played do not establish Infinite Treadmill, and the failed updates and connection delay are technical faults rather than a dark-pattern mechanic. No other described behavior meets a label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "paid a lot for extra coins and things",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency / boundary rule: the currency is the object acquired with real-world payment.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes paying money to acquire extra in-game coins."
